window.docContents[13].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Lesson":{"contents":"1. Bare reals. Put oscillator B's mass in oscillator A's frequency radicand. The\nupstream oscillator accepts the cross-oscillator expression as a real:\n\n\n\n2. Kinded, but bearer-free. The spring-constant/mass quotient also accepts values from\ndifferent oscillators. It is still a Quantity, with no oscillator index:\n\n\n\n3. Declare the context. Oscillators A and B are distinct particulars of one sort.\nEach contains one body and one wall spring; neither component is separately indexed yet:\n\n\n\n4. Designate without collapsing. A designation gives each oscillator a nominal name.\nDesignated requires a proof, designation_inj, that the designation is injective: two\ndifferent oscillators never get the same name. Without that proof, anything keyed by name,\nsuch as a report or a SysML export, could merge A and B into one system. This is the\nfalse-identity risk. The instance below names A \"oscillator A\" and B \"oscillator B\" and\nproves injectivity by cases. The theorem constant_designation_not_injective shows why a\nlazy designation fails: the function that names every oscillator \"oscillator\" sends A and\nB to the same name, so it is not injective, and no Designated instance can use it:\n\n\n\n5. Carry the bearer. The oscillator is the whole characterized by ω₀; mass and\nstiffness therefore share its index. One Rig name is one system: two TwoOscillators r\nrecords are its states at different times, configurations, or rival estimates, and\ncomparing their quantities is the point. OscillatorOf r keeps those records' oscillator\nbearers shared; different rig names are distinct systems regardless of matching parameters.\nUnder L0's\nmassless-spring, fixed-wall assumptions, these magnitudes read the body's mass and\nspring's stiffness. The existing kind table supplies the quotient law; the magnitude\ntheorem reads the real fields. The catalogue entry remains shared because both oscillators\nhave the same sort:\n\n\n\nThe sort-level catalogue comparison uses Sorted Oscillator; the rig-scoped wrapper\nneeds no additional Sorted or Designated instance.\n\n6. Compare states; refuse cross-system substitution. Two weighings of oscillator A at\ndifferent times on rig R₁ have a typed mass difference whose magnitude reads the later\nand earlier real fields. B's mass cannot fill A's slot, A's stiffness cannot be divided by\nB's mass, and the two different oscillators' quantities cannot be added at this layer. Nor\ncan same-named oscillator A masses be added across R₁ and R₂: those names identify\ndistinct systems. Total mass is physically meaningful, but whose total requires a whole;\nthat is L2's question:\n\n\n\n\n\n7. A name is not a sort. A nominal Object does not supply Sorted System, so the\ncatalogue cannot be dedicated through that name:\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies","header":"5.2.7. Lesson","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Lesson"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Summary-of-the-differences-in-meaning":{"contents":"A reference that cites ISO 80000-1 or the VIM for a term and this work for its formal\ndefinition should take account of these:\n\n* KindOfProperty and PropertyValue are broader than kind of quantity and\nquantity value: they include the nominal case. Restrict them by IsQuantity and\nIsQuantityValue to obtain the ISO/VIM concepts.* Quantity k R is narrower than quantity: it is a kind-indexed magnitude. The\ngeneral quantity is the kind, and the individual quantity is IndividualQuantity.* MetrologicalUnit is a reference to a kind with a symbol, not a quantity.* PropertyValue.numeral and measure hold an integer, and numeral is a numerical\nvalue in the VIM sense only at interval and ratio kinds.* PropertyValue.Comparable requires equal kinds, and MutuallyComparable requires a\ncommon declared ancestor and is not transitive.* KindOfProperty.IsDifferential, IsRational, ScaleType.AllowsDifference and\nAllowsRatio have a defined term only in VIM 4 2CD.* Carrier, LawfulCarrier, Quantity.instDecidableLE, LowerBound, UpperBound and\nthe three Comparable laws have no counterpart in any of the ISO or VIM documents.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Standards Reference — PKC beside ISO 80000-1 and the VIM","header":"34.9. Summary of the differences in meaning","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Summary-of-the-differences-in-meaning"}});